        <title type="html"><![CDATA[Chrome 144 adds Linux WebGPU support]]></title>
        <id>https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/28773</id>
        <link href="https://github.com/mdn/browser-compat-data/pull/28773"/>
        <updated>2026-01-08T10:18:21.000Z</updated>
        <content type="html"><![CDATA[<img src="https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/47647?u=2fbfc431a85d357f00c0146d5e55b425b7f00a3d&v=4" width="64" height="64" alt=""/><br/>
<h4 dir="auto">Summary</h4>
<p dir="auto">Chrome 144 adds Linux WebGPU support, currently only for Intel Gen12+ GPUs. See <a href="https://developer.chrome.com/blog/new-in-webgpu-144?hl=en#webgpu_on_linux" rel="nofollow">https://developer.chrome.com/blog/new-in-webgpu-144?hl=en#webgpu_on_linux</a>.</p>
<p dir="auto">Eventually, this PR aims to add data updates for all WebGPU features; however, for the moment, I am just adding a single data point update so we can make sure we are happy with the wording before updating it everywhere.</p>
<p dir="auto">The Chrome team had a related question: Could we remove <code class="notranslate">partial_implementation</code> from the 144 data? It is now supported on all platforms, but there will always be platforms/configs that won't be supported for GPUs.</p>
<p dir="auto">cc <a class="user-mention notranslate" data-hovercard-type="user" data-hovercard-url="/users/beaufortfrancois/hovercard" data-octo-click="hovercard-link-click" data-octo-dimensions="link_type:self" href="https://github.com/beaufortfrancois">@beaufortfrancois</a></p>

